## Research Question 2
Research Question:
Within the set of fatal crashes, are crashes more common on weekends than weekdays, and do the timing and types of vehicles involved differ between weekend and weekday crashes?

### Create Weekend Variable

The following code creates a new variable that classifies crashes as either occurring on a weekday or a weekend.

```{r}
accident_weekend <- accident_small |>
  mutate(
    weekend = if_else(
      DAY_WEEKNAME %in% c("Saturday", "Sunday"),
      "Weekend",
      "Weekday"))
```
```{r}
accident_weekend |>
  count(weekend)
```

### Plot 1: Average Fatal Crashes per Day
```{r}

accident_weekend |>
  count(weekend) |>
  mutate(
    days = if_else(weekend == "Weekday", 5, 2),
    crashes_per_day = n / days
  ) |>
  ggplot(aes(x = weekend, y = crashes_per_day)) +
  geom_col(fill = "steelblue") +
  geom_text(
    aes(label = round(crashes_per_day, 1)),
    vjust = -0.5,
    size = 5
  ) +
  labs(
    title = "Average Fatal Crashes per Day",
    x = "Day Type",
    y = "Average Fatal Crashes per Day"
  ) +
  scale_y_continuous(
    limits = c(0, 6500),
    expand = expansion(mult = c(0, 0.02))
  ) +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 14)
```
#To account for the different number of weekdays and weekend days, fatal crashes were standardized as the average number of crashes per day. On average, weekends had more fatal crashes per day than weekdays, suggesting that fatal crashes occurred more frequently on a typical weekend day.


### Plot 2a: Distribution of Fatal Crashes by Hour

```{r}
