## Research Question 3
Research Question:
For fatal crashes, which state, crash, vehicle, and driver characteristics are most strongly associated with whether a crash occurs on a weekend and at night versus a weekday and/or daytime?


## Plot 1: Fatal Crashes During Day and Night
```{r}
accident_daynight <- accident_small |>
  mutate(
    day_night = if_else(
      LGT_CONDNAME == "Daylight",
      "Day",
      "Night"
    )
  )
```
```{r}


accident_daynight |>
  ggplot(aes(x = day_night)) +
  geom_bar(fill = "steelblue") +
  labs(
    title = "Number of Fatal Crashes During Day and Night",
    x = "Time of Day",
    y = "Number of Fatal Crashes"
  ) +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 14)
```

## Plot 2: Driver Alcohol Involvement by Day and Night


```{r}
driver_daynight <- driver_small |>
  left_join(
    accident_daynight |>
      select(ST_CASE, day_night),
    by = "ST_CASE"
  ) |>
  mutate(
    alcohol_involvement = case_when(
      DRINKINGNAME == "Yes (Alcohol Involved)" ~ "Alcohol Involved",
      DRINKINGNAME == "No (Alcohol Not Involved)" ~ "No Alcohol Involved",
      TRUE ~ NA_character_
    )
  )
```

```{r}


driver_daynight |>
  filter(!is.na(alcohol_involvement)) |>
  ggplot(
    aes(
      x = day_night,
      fill = alcohol_involvement
    )
  ) +
  geom_bar(position = "fill") +
  scale_y_continuous(labels = scales::percent) +
  labs(
    title = "Driver Alcohol Involvement During Day and Night",
    x = "Time of Day",
    y = "Percentage of Drivers",
    fill = "Alcohol Involvement"
  ) +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 14)
```
#The proportion of drivers involved with alcohol was substantially higher at night than during the day. Approximately 29% of nighttime drivers were classified as alcohol involved, compared with about 10% of daytime drivers. This suggests that alcohol involvement may be an important characteristic associated with nighttime fatal crashes.


